  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「カシワ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AB%E3%82%B7%E3%83%AF",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 111188586,
      "text": "カシワ は、ブナ科コナラ属の落葉高木。日本・朝鮮半島・中国の東アジア地域に分布しており、痩せ地でも生育し、海岸で群落になっているところもある。葉は、かつて料理を盛るために使われ、端午の節句の柏餅を包む葉としても知られる。冬でも葉が落葉せずに枝に残ることから、日本では神が宿る縁起木とされている。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Quercus dentata」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quercus_dentata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1363975263,
      "text": "Quercus dentata, also called Japanese emperor oak, daimyo oak, or Korean oak, is a species of oak native to East Asia, including north-central and southern China, Japan, Korea, the Russian Far East, Manchuria, Mongolia, and Taiwan. The name of the tree is often translated as \"sweet oak\" in English to distinguish it from Western varieties. It is placed in section Quercus."
    }
  },
